Date: Tue, 4 May 1993 10:28:55 -0600
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: Meta: Judgment Metzger vs. Moore

>We create this trouble in part by not making it explicit enough. This
>discussion should motivate the Board to act to make it more clearcut.
>Freeman Craig Presson

I agree that this should be made quite clear in the welcome statement. 
Harry is now working on a new one that does so.

The Board will probably soon ratify a List Code that also makes Harry's
powers and responsibilities clear, and that Code will be sent to all list
members.  This debate has proven very useful, as I am gleening it to see if
the current version of the Code should be modified to account for the
objections presented.

So far, though, I've seen few objections that won't be greatly alleviated
by having a clear description of the powers and duties of the List
Administrator and the List Adjudicator.  There is only one real problem
area: it looks as if anyone can bring suit for disruption of the list but
that only the disputants can appeal the Administrator's decisions in such
suits.

Since everyone loses from flaming (not just the parties flamed) and many
offenses don't directly target any one particular person (such as when
someone posts lewd and irrelevant messages), I think it is probably best to
let anyone bring suit for violation of the Code.  Should anyone be allowed,
then, to appeal the Administrator's decisions?  This seems like a
reasonable measure, but I suspect it may increase litigation more than it
corrects questionable decisions.

I would like to see something like the English rule applied to list
proceedings: if someone brings frivolous suits, they should suffer some
sort of sanction.  Otherwise there is no disincentive to litigating
willy-nilly.  Improper litigation unfairly burdens the Administrator and
Adjudicator, and degrades what should be a friendly and open atmosphere.


