WARNING: This is a LONG message!
*******************************************************************
From: "Christopher L. Weeks" <C576653@MIZZOU1.missouri.edu>

>First, a question;  Are you saying that, using your view of ethics, ethics of
>the norm are somehow universally correct?  Is it just plain wrong to steal, or
>are you just saying that the majority of people believe that it is?

The latter, definately.  I don't believe in absolutes of that sort.  Actually,
it would better be phrased that it seems to me, on the basis of my experience
and readings, that the majority of cultures have a certain set of social
mores and behaviour codes that are if not universal, very nearly so, and that
they mostly also have another set of widely differing codes that are local
to the group (add on top of this a variety of individual codes.)  So, I don't 
think it really has much to do with "the majority of people" but rather socie-
tal structures.  I dare say, a plain majority of people in this country believe
in a moral code, but I think the difference between what I call ethics and 
morals is still very clear cut and plain in our culture as elsewhere.

>I have commonly heard the definition of morals vs. ethics as going something
>like;  Morals relate to what the individual FEELS is right while ethics relate
>to how others, in one's society, percieve one's behavior.  This definition
>seems to sort-of go with the one given.

Hmmm...well, given that I did not take this argument very far (testing reactions
first, to see if there is enough interest to warrant working on my essay), I
can see how you might see that, but I really don't think the 2 views are all
that compatible.  Both ethics and morals seem to me to be social phenomena,
while personal codes might be something entirely different.  I'd say both
e. and m. are a product of "how others...perceive one's behavior", and that
the distinction lies in their universality and arbitrariness.  Not eating
pork for Jews would seem to be a moral, not an ethic, for instance, while not
cutting the eyes out of your children would be an ethic (again stressing that
these are my own definitions, not necessarily those of conventional philosophy,
which in many cases uses the terms interchangably).  I certainly agree that
there is a difference between personal perceptions of what "FEELS" right, and
what a group or society mandates is correct behaviour.

>I'm also curious, being philisophically illiterate, if there is some standard
>solution or set of definitions which most people follow.

Not sure what you mean...solution to what?  Definitions of what?

**********************************************************
From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)

>I find Stanton's definitions of morals and ethics rather useful; they
>conveniently allow me to restate my position. I hope the distinction
>suggested by Stanton is generally acceptable. 

I am actually surprised most of the responses to the idea are so positive.
In private chats with people, most of them thought it was bunk.  Must've
been talking to the wrong folks I guess.  >:)

>Morals are subjective opinions on what is 'right' and 'wrong', determined
>by culture, theology etc. They are totally unextropian, and have no
>objective existence. 

Gotta agree with that! MOST unextropian.  That is why my teeth grate often
when reading this list, because I will see people bandering about morals or
aguments about morals as if they were all-earth universals, and I don't see
that they are.  However, it seems to me that ethics are also "determined
by culture", or rather are an integral part of culture, whereas morals are
not, and tend to vary over time and geography, even from household to house-
hold.  All this conservative lamenting about how the "moral fiber" of America
is eroding, etc etc, sort of proves the point.  Almost all of the things being
lamented are things that by their very nature change, and have nothing close
to universality (work "ethic"; "traditional family values"; acceptance of
non-conservative behaviour, dress, sexual preference; etc., ad nauseum.)

>As Mike Price says, absolute morals is a meaningless
>concept. The moral relativists are totally right (although moral
>absolutists must be using another definition).

I don't know about "totally right", but the main point seems sound to me.
Don't s'pose you know just what that other definition is?  I have not read
enough such material to be able to judge it myself, but it seems to me the
main flaw in their reasoning is that they try to elevate (what I call)
morals to the level of ethics [if that is too judgemental, because I imply
ethics are "better" than morals, try: "they try to force morals into the
wholly separate realm of ethics, and the shoe does not fit"], often by using
"reasons" that have no logical, but much emotional/propagandal, force, such as
"because Christ said so in John 12:1", "it's unAmerican any other way", 
etc.

>On the other hand, there is an absolute code of ethics that I believe all
>civilised advanced intelligent life would voluntarily conform to. Do not
>kill or initiate force without provocation. 

This is interesting, and one of the things that I hoped would be brought up.
One of the points of my proto-essay is ethics tend to vary *by degree*, 
rather than by detail.  For instance (to get back on track here), what 
constitutes "provocation"?  I think you'd find this is highly variable;
in our mainstream culture killing another requires extreme provocation,
(or a badge, heh) to be "gotten away with".  In one of the tribal societies
of Papua New Guinea, simply being a member of an opposing tribe is quite
provocation enough for bloodshed.  Yet the ethic still holds.  Among their
own village, violence is uncommon.

>What if a society placed a
>very low value on life? This seems extremely unlikely except in primitive,
>barbaric cultures. And in any case, shouldn't it be the right of the
>individual to decide how greatly he / she values life and good health,
>rather than the primitive society making the decision on behalf of its
>members?

Well to digress a bit, I'd suggest that that is an ideal, but not necessarily
a "truth" (whatever that is).  I think our own modern, civilized culture has
a pretty low esteem for life...and the notion that cultures that are not of
the "modern" Western mold are "primitive, barbaric cultures" has been largely
tossed out the window.  Certainly many cultures at least SEEM to be more
violent than ours, but then again the Trobriand islanders didn't invent
the nuclear bomb or the gas chamber or the machine gun.  Kinda balances out.
Such cultures MIGHT be more "personally" (in-your-face) violent, but our 
culture has killed more people than any other in history (by "our" I mean
European, though I suppose that is more of a metaculture.)

>So this code of ethics is somehow absolute, an eternal Truth rooted in the
>nature of civilised intelligent life and the universe. Whenever a violation
>occurs, justice has not prevailed.

Not sure *I* am willing to go that far, but...  For one thing, we know nothing
about any sort of intelligent life but ourselves, unless one wishes to include
other primates, and dolphins/whales.  And for another, how can something that
is a human code of behaviour have anything to do with eternal Truths of the
universe.  Not saying it doesn't but I am asking for a clarification/expansion
of this idea before I am ready to accept (or reject) it. 

*********************************************************
habs@panix.com (Harry Shapiro):

>I like to think that Morals are based on religious theology and are
>very un-Extropian. (And GOD said, "Thou shall not repost Extropian
>messages.")

>I like to think that Ethics are community based standards for
>behavior. We don't allow reposting of Extropian messages without the
>authors permission.  

Not meaning to sound like a know it all, I'd suggest that there are a few
problems with that, namely that not all morals [here I am assuming you are
playing along with my definitions? It is hard to tell] are religiously
based, and that many moral arguments are based on community values as well.
I'd agree that MOST morals stem from religion, but that many grow out of it.
Our culture's widespread homophobia stems from Christian dogma, but has 
spread to plenty irreligious, even atheist, people including scientists.
The moral judgement that S&M is bad but "regular" sex is ok would seem to
me to have little to do with religion, but a lot to do with community mores.
Anway, I think there is something of import about the distinction you are
making here, but I can't quite place it.  I would agree with you quite strongly
that the "GOD" version of the reposting policy is certainly a moral argument,
and that the "we have agreed as a group" version is ethical, but I am not sure
the reasons WHY add up.  Again, not saying you are full of it but rather that
something is missing, and I am asking as much as telling.

>There can also be personal extensions to community Ethics (e.g., The
>community allows you to do X, but you decide not to do it (e.g., Tim
>May not being willing for file charges in this community even though
>it is ethically ok.).

Good point. I'm trying to think of a counter-example from the moral side.
Even besides absurdities (e.g. American morals allow you to draw a smiley
face on your belly button, but refusing to do so is not evidence of extensi-
bility...), and lots come to mind.  To sort of turn this around I'd suggest
that both POSITIVE morals and ethics are not obligatory, but negative ones
are, and that (I may be reaching here) morals are quite extensible (i.e.
one can make up all sorts of arbitrary rules -- thou shalt wear a veil, thou
shalt not lick another's genitals, etc etc), whereas ethics are not (I don't
think I can PROVE that, but just a gedankenexperiment with it yields no fruit;
I can think of nothing that one could add to ethics that would carry the weight
of ethics.  Like I was saying before, I think that ethics vary by DEGREE and
by such things as "what applies and what does not?", while morals vary widely
and wildly.  I'd have to say that our reposting policy comes from the ethic 
that one does not steal from one's own.  We have simply decided that "what
applies" includes our electronic written "speech".  If you are skeptical,
take a look at how much material cannibalized from magazines gets posted
here.  It isn't from our own, so it is ok.  This sort of thing may well be
quite subconscious.  

>There may be Ethical standards that can be "discovered" through
>experiments (like game theory), but I don't give this much credit.

If that means what I think it does, I don't see why not. IF as many of these
ethics (and morals) are as subconscious as I think they are, it may take quite
a bit of inquiry to "discover" them.  If you mean new ones are essentially
created in some way by such study, then yeah, I don't give it much credit
either.  Could you clarify?

>I think market forces (in a PPL community) will tend to set the most
>effective "basket" of ethical standards.

Hmmm...I honestly don't know enough about things like market forces (especially
PPL and extropian definitions of them) to really comment, other than I am 
quite skeptical of trying to explain non-economic things in terms of economics.
Not sure that is what you are doing, so again, I hope you can clarify.

