>of ethics is defined mostly as the science of conduct.  The word "morals"
>is used synonymously, with the exception that since it's of Anglo-Saxon
>derivation, those who aren't up to speaking in words of Classical origin
>tend to speak of morals, rather than ethics.

Hmm to be argumentative [imagine that!], I am not so sure that being up to
it is the reason, more like general consensus usage. Moral is most often used
in religous circles, and ethic in professional (especially legal and medical)
circles.  I almost resent the implication that usage of OE-rooted words is
somehow the mark of ignorance, but I don't QUITE resent it, since it is true
that the ignorant tend to use fewer Latinate words (that is, there is a 
correlation, but I think it is one-way; just because someone doesn't use
Latinate words, doesn't mean they are ignorant).  But then again, I have always
had a hard time with the idea that the only real education is one based on 
"Classical" subjects.  Just calling Graeco-Roman culture "Classical" demon-
strates a bias.  BUT ANYWAY...

Yeah, I see what you mean.  I think, as I said, part of the Korzybskian dilemma
