Dear Extropians,

I read with much interest your recent discussions of Ayn Rand.  I think
that there are a great many misconceptions about what Objectivists are
up to these days.  One particularly striking example is the accusation
that "Objectivists are encouraged to smoke."  This is simply untrue.  Most
of us are passionate about our health and do not smoke.  Furthermore, I
am unaware of any social pressure to smoke in Objectivist groups.  I
wasn't around in the 1960s, and whatever happened with Rand and her
personal life seems patently irrelevant today.

I don't know that much about Extropians or the Extropy Institute, but
from what I gather from reading this list and from browsing a single copy
of a magazine I think was called _Exponent_ (is that it?), Extropians
count Ayn Rand as one major influence.  In particular, I think that her
reality-orientation and her biocentric ethics are of particular interest
to Extropians.

Anyhow, here is an advertisement for my e-mail list for the discussion
of Ayn Rand's ideas.  I am not affiliated in any way with the Ayn Rand
Institute.  Many of my list members would happily accept the label
"David Kelley fans."  If you are aware of the internal controversies
within the Objectivist movement, you will know that we are the people
who are the best hope for the furtherance of Objectivism as serious
philosophy.

*****************************************************************
*      The Moderated Discussion of Objectivist Philosophy       *
*****************************************************************
*                   A Polite E-mail Forum                       *
*****************************************************************

Ayn Rand was one of the most popular and controversial thinkers
of the 20th century.  Her books have sold in the millions and
continue to sell over 400,000 copies per year.  In a recent poll
taken by the Library of Congress and the Book-of-the-Month club,
'regular readers' were asked to name the single book that had
the most impact upon their lives.  Ayn Rand's _Atlas Shrugged_
came in second, beaten only by the Christian Bible.  A movie
version of _Atlas Shrugged_ will be produced soon, an event that
should help to fuel the continuing interest in her work.  In
short, her impact on our culture has been undeniably profound.

Her novels and philosophical works offer a vision of reality and
of human life that is at once heroic and realistic.  An
uncompromising advocate of reason, life, happiness, egoism, and
capitalism, she is perhaps the single philosopher who best
captures what is unique about the sense of life of the United
States of America.

Evidence of her impact can be found all around us here on the
computer networks.  Lists devoted to general philosophical
discussion are routinely full of discussions (most often heated
and angry from both sides) about her work and what it means.
Unfortunately, these kinds of discussions are not generally
productive, since too many pseudo-experts are far too ready to
express dramatic opinions pro and con.  Those interested in
reading serious discussions of Rand's philosophical ideas will
not be happy with these unmoderated forums.

But there is an alternative.  "The Moderated Discussion of
Objectivist Philosophy" is an active but fairly low volume
discussion of Ayn Rand's specifically philosophical ideas.  The
list is moderated and organized, so that discussions are always
polite and always focussed on some particular point of interest.
The list specializes in the specifically _philosophical_ ideas of
Ayn Rand (including her contributions in metaphysics, epistemology,
ethics, politics, and aesthetics) as opposed to her impact on our
culture or her colorful personal life.

To my knowledge, this list is the highest quality discussion of
philosophical ideas anywhere in the entire computer world.  By
pursuing a standard of excellence and politeness generally unknown
on the networks, I have managed to attract a number of top scholars
in the field to our discussions.

YOU ARE INVITED!  Everyone is welcome to subscribe to the list
and there is absolutely no charge for subscription.  This list
is not designed to be a forum for those who would take Rand's
ideas to be true on blind faith, and polite and well-reasoned
dissent is welcome.  I have never turned down any posts for
publication based on disagreement with Rand's ideas.  The only
standard I impose for postings is that every post be well-written,
polite, well-reasoned, and that postings exhibit at least a basic
understanding of Rand's ideas.  I'm an extraordinarily polite and
reasonable person, so that even discussions of as controversial
a figure as Rand have proceeded smoothly for over 2 years now.

Come join us, and see what all the fuss is about!

